[Music]  what does it take to change the world a  breakthrough idea an unparalleled  background in technology and execution  rich wealthy connections to Stanford  investors over my 10 years of experience  in consulting then start of financing  and high tech in Silicon Valley I've  become convinced that we are profoundly  mistaken in our conventional wisdom and  what it takes to make breakthrough  technology succeed and to truly change  the world  it took me a moment of meeting the  Google X co-founder face to face to  realize that the techniques that  astrophysicists and laser scientists are  using in Silicon Valley's most  innovative laboratories are simple  repeatable steps that anyone with access  to basic supplies can start doing at  home right away  and even more excitingly if we were to  really realize this as a society our  communities companies and classrooms  would never be the same  my journey toward learning more about  innovation began when I was a young girl  in Topeka Kansas when I was young my  greatest hope for the future of  technology would be that we would invent  a better remote control and my greatest  hope for government was that we'd find a  way to keep the streets swept  particularly in the snow while  minimizing taxes for my dad's small  business growing up in Kansas I never  imagined that a non engineer could work  for a company like Google but in 2010 I  found out in fact they could and after a  year of getting my ducks in a row I  applied for Google and I landed my first  job there very quickly into my time at  Google I got the opportunity to help  build tech support teams to help make  Gmail and other Google Apps usable by  business  is large and small around the world we  started off trying to train people from  many cultures how to answer a simple  tech question on the phone and resolve  it so the customer could move on with  their day but over time we started to  realize that this awesome staff we'd  built weren't just tech support agents  in fact they were very skillful very  creative humans who could do a lot more  for the humans on the other side of the  phone than we'd originally thought at  first we started small helping new  customers go from fixing an early  problem to anticipating what might  happen next then if someone called with  one question we try and help figure out  what was actually going on that they  were trying to use technology to solve  and then finally we realized that if we  were really effective at helping our  customers on the phone we could help  them bring technology to bear on their  work force in their company to power  present and future goals that they found  important the most amazing learning for  me in this process was actually the  innovation doesn't have to happen only  for teams that are developing software  in fact when you can teach people to  innovate whose work involves other  people amazing things can happen and  things can transform with this Small's  intervention as one interaction on the  phone after I left Google it wasn't long  before I found myself in a very strange  classroom in an innovation mansion on  the border of Alamo Square in San  Francisco I stepped through the  beautiful entryway looked at the grand  staircase coming down from above and  followed red rope lights into a  mysterious basement that turned out to  be a very curious place indeed here Tom  Chi a co-founder of Google X with  unveiling the methods that Google X's  engineers had used to develop  breakthrough technologies that put in  the internet on floating balloons I was  very excited and I was especially  enthusiastic because the one thing our  team  I figured out how to do was to solve a  bottleneck and an engineering talent  basically how do we get more technical  skills and technical people to help  bring our customer insights into real  opportunities and tools for our  customers it's Tom begin to share he  talked about how a lot of the world's  wealth runs through its largest  companies but companies don't actually  develop all of the technology that they  create and whether they develop it or  not they tend to own the patents for 20  more years what I started to realize is  we couldn't just trust that the world's  big companies would have a handle on the  innovation that we really need to solve  the problems we want to see solved in  our lifetime instead it was going to  take a lot of people working together  really quickly in order to tackle  problems on the scale of climate change  and we were going to need to get started  right away as tom shared the method I  started to become astonished because  what I imagined would be a highly math  intensive engineering specific  discipline really boil down to three  steps that Google X's engineers use over  and over and over to reduce uncertainty  and bring the reality bring the present  in the future into present reality step  one start with ask it step to build  something basic using pretty accessible  kindergarten level supplies and step  three changes you go again and again  they repeated this process and in the  room we use post-its and scissors to do  the same thing start with a skit build  something basic change as you go start  with the skit build something basic  change as you go the more we've repeated  this process the more it started to sink  in it wasn't just my team at Google that  had had everything we needed to move  forward on building technology in our  corporate context but actually the  classrooms I had grown up in in Kansas  had everything we needed then too I was  infuriated  not sure where I got the message that in  order to create the future I needed  access to the best science and  technology labs and teachers who had 20  years of experience in data science but  at this point I started to realize that  if we all truly believed in this  potential a lot could change and a lot  more people could be involved in  directing that change the next phase of  my career was a 3-year stint testing my  hypothesis that this was a basic process  that anybody could learn I taught people  from 50 organizations from the US  Department of Labor to Girl Scouts  at a science and tech camp I taught  corporate entrepreneurial and nonprofit  sector leaders from Norway to Vietnam  and including the American Midwest and  the biggest insight I had from that  journey was a workshop I taught two  undergrads in central Iowa the workshop  began and everyone started and very  quickly as I do I said let's get going  time to build right yay and this young  woman named Chelsea looked up at me and  said I can't do it I can't go forward  and I said why not she looked at me and  said you're asking me to create and no  one's ever even told me I can be  creative that's when I realized that  there are thousands of creators besides  my original self who don't even realize  that we can create and in fact we are  creating all the time but our own lack  of awareness that that's what we're  already up to and it's absolutely us who  can do it holds us back from really  making headway on the things we care  about the most  what I've learned over the course of  this journey is that imagination and  invention aren't hyper specific  technologies that no one can use it's  that actually it's more like imagination  is a muscle it's a process that anybody  can pick up that you can exercise and  develop and by  to sing in this method you can begin to  make real progress on the things you  care about and your community cares  about the most and you can empower your  workforce and your economy to do exactly  the same if we really live like  imagination as a muscle you all could  make a huge difference even in the rest  of this week maybe you would start by  looking around you for people with wild  ideas who really could just grab a piece  of scrap paper and start to draw what  the wireframe interface would look like  rather than applying to another  accelerator or worse yet quitting if  they don't get in you could look around  at the groups the communities and the  workforce is that you are a part of four  places where actually a lot more as  possible than humans just doing routine  activities and if you did you might find  out that the unemployment graph flipped  starts to look like capacity to innovate  and you could put your ear to the ground  for some innovators either likely or  unlikely who might not yet have been  told that in fact they too can be  creative returning to your routine today  I invite you to start this process by  keeping an open mind for maybe a project  that you haven't been able to move  forward yet is there something that you  can just put on a rough sheet of paper  and hand to a friend to test if you did  I think you might discover that you are  far more inventive even very rapidly  than you ever dared to think  [Applause]

